{"kind":"request","text":"Typed definitions: coverage of the contract, the live Counter, and the\nconsolidation below the library. Amends DX-T1 da58095a and DX-T2 aeec9280.\n\nSource: Hugh's questions of 28 October 2026, after the spike: what of the\ndefinition and the application the typed language leaves out, what form\neach should take, and whether the downstream interfaces already read the\nmaterialized formats. Hugh asked that the answer go to the planner. The\nmeasurements are from main 0881c1bc on 28 October, counted on the\ncanonical JSON of each definition.\n\n1. The live Counter is not the one the spike re-expressed.\n   The installed Counter package (apps/counter/dap.application.json) pins\n   apps/counter/commitments.json, definition counting-commitments, digest\n   b55824346382d9b158be4cadd9d098008f8129147fba772537778e2f738d5271.\n   The spike re-expressed apps/counter/definition.json (counting,\n   435d9f57), which no package installs. DX-T1 item 2 should name\n   commitments.json. It uses four forms the spike's builders cannot\n   write: unset, anyOf, before, settles. The counting rewrite can stay as\n   a witness; it costs nothing to keep.\n\n2. Coverage of the declared contract (packages/contract/src/definition.ts).\n   The spike's builders cover the forms lending and counting use and\n   little else. Forms outside them, and who uses them:\n\n   Definition          Bytes   Acts  Handlers  Forms outside the builders\n   lending              4,604     6      0     none\n   counting (old)      15,124     9      0     none\n   counting-commitments 12,692   14      0     unset, anyOf, before, settles\n   grow-room           38,528    31      0     set, unset, anyOf, fact guard,\n                                               after, before, ifPresent, via,\n                                               part reads, capability guard\n                                               and effect, hold, relate\n   grow-site            4,198     5      1     create send, a handler, sender\n                                               and source operands\n   issue               46,160    50      7     most of the contract\n   change              59,715    54      4     most of the contract\n\n   Not yet built, by group: guards notIn, set, unset, every, fact, before,\n   after, each, has, anyOf, distinct, capability, and the ifPresent and\n   reason modifiers; effects party list add and remove, attribute, hold,\n   redact, capability; sends create, relate, index, and the collect\n   source; attention (notify); handlers (receives) with their late, order\n   and copies rows; act presents and settles; field and value default;\n   party list, max and author; slot capability; detached text; digest,\n   commit and tree types; operands presented, sender, source, update,\n   result, scope, intent, none, and part reads; definition level and\n   capacity (no live definition sets them; define should accept them).\n\n   Asked: DX-T1 builds the whole declared contract, or the planner files\n   DX-T3 for the remainder with the grow room and site as its witness\n   (both compile to their current digests) and the lane definitions as\n   the final witness. The reason modifier gets a named wrapper, which is\n   where DX-B1 (8a1bc098) declared refusal names land. A receive builder\n   gets its own subject vocabulary for sender, source and update. A\n   create send takes a typed reference to another definition's value.\n\n3. The four forms, as the design rule for the package.\n   Builders compile into the pinned bytes: every contract form.\n   Derivations are computed from the value and never authored: the pin,\n   projection field types, forms, acts available by state, expected\n   revisions, act sentences, input schemas, documentation tables.\n   Annotations are typed sidecars keyed by the definition's literal\n   names and compiled into the package presentation, never into the pin:\n   labels, units and scale, refusal words, view templates, carousel\n   titles, human descriptions. The definition carries no prose by\n   design and the MCP description derivation depends on that.\n   Code is typed by the definition: the shelf fold, the voice\n   controller, later policies and reactors. The grow definitions already\n   use a third hand-rolled helper style beside the Counter's helpers and\n   the lanes' shared rows; the package replaces all three.\n   Platform data stays out: platform definitions with code marks, the\n   membership role lists, the destination and rules scopes. The library\n   types references to them (scope kinds, tell message names) and does\n   not author them.\n\n4. Rule expressions: a tagged template, not an expression tree.\n   Amends DX-T2 item 3. Keep the restricted language as written and\n   check the names: interpolations are typed field and slot references\n   that compile to the profile's paths. A full builder of the JSONata\n   subset is more work and a second language to learn.\n\n5. Downstream interfaces already read the pinned bytes. Keep that.\n   Observed: packages/mcp/src/describe.ts derives the act sentence,\n   field words, guard sentences and the tool input schema from the act\n   declaration, total over the contract's forms with a totality test;\n   catalogue.ts makes one tool per act of each active definition with\n   identity by declaration digest; views/declared.ts interprets the\n   package presentation and derives one model tool per parameterized\n   control; dap check and pack validate package bytes, run the package\n   graph and the scenarios; projection and view binding check every name\n   and type against the pinned definition; the room page takes its forms\n   from act.fields and its revisions from the CLI browser helpers;\n   docs/lanes-reference.md and docs/mcp-tools.json are generated from\n   the declarations. None of these should take a TypeScript dependency.\n   The scope pins bytes, the host interprets bytes, the package is\n   signed bytes, replay reads bytes. The library compiles to those bytes.\n\n6. What should refactor is duplication, not the trust boundary.\n   a. One expected-revision derivation. Four implementations today:\n      packages/mcp/src/shape.ts expectedOf (the fullest: one, by, via,\n      code), packages/cli/src/browser-helpers.ts expectedOf (a near\n      duplicate), apps/counter/browser/model.ts (inline over act.also),\n      and the spike's room.ts. Hand-written revisions in at least five\n      more places: the lending browser model, lending and grow\n      test-support, and every scenario JSON ($revision). Put the full\n      one in the derive or client package; DX-T2 item 1 uses it rather\n      than adding a fifth.\n   b. Move the description derivations (actSentence, typeWords,\n      fieldLines, guardSentences, actInputSchema) out of packages/mcp\n      into a shared package. DX-T2 item 2's formFor is that derivation,\n      not a second one; the spike's formFor and the CLI browser helpers'\n      describe are weaker copies and go.\n   c. A projection builder that derives each field's of and nullable\n      rows from the typed value. Today the manifest copies the slot type\n      verbatim and bindApplicationProjection refuses any difference, so\n      deriving them changes no bytes. The author chooses items, states,\n      limits and which slots to read.\n   d. Views typed from the projection, so acts, needs, facts and template\n      names are checked in the editor, and when goes through the same\n      tagged template. Binding is unchanged.\n   e. Roles declared first, and grant typed as the union of the declared\n      actions, so the package graph's ungranted-act is a compile error.\n   f. Scenario JSON generated from a typed story (DX-B2 3770c687); the\n      runner and the pinned JSON are unchanged.\n   g. The lending and Counter browser models typed from the definition\n      value, with their act lists, byte limits and inline proposals\n      deleted; refusal words become an annotation table keyed by act\n      and reason, typed when DX-B1 lands.\n   h. Package derivation and parameters (unsupported today, read.ts says\n      they belong to P5) describe what typed composition does: the\n      spike's due-date variant is a new definition with its own digest.\n      Build one mechanism. The library can emit the parent digest and\n      the forms added as provenance for the package to carry later.\n\nAsked of the planner: attach items 1, 2 and 4 to DX-T1 and DX-T2 by\nsupersede, or file DX-T3 (remaining contract forms, grow and lanes\nwitnesses), DX-T4 (manifest builders: projections, views, roles) and a\nconsolidation item for 6a, 6b and 6g; sequence 6c to 6f with DX-T4. The\nkernel is unchanged. No filing-gate item is opened.","body":{"conditions":"Planner attaches to DX-T1 da58095a and DX-T2 aeec9280 by supersede, or files the children named inside (DX-T3, DX-T4, consolidation); closes by landing of those children or by a planner supersede naming the request that carries each item.","target_head":"06e4d33fe17937610891f29b5d1513b9e224edea","target_ref":"refs/heads/main","target_repo":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3","to":"20db43a245e9780b92a587425d387d393b17ffc90b89e036ff752399f88fc517"}}